# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 001
ps("V02-A-001", "Philosophy and ethics", "A promise binds against inconvenience but not against circumstances it was never meant to cover.", "Easy",
   "A promise binds us partly because it lets another person plan around what we will do. That is why a promise cannot simply be cancelled when keeping it becomes inconvenient: the inconvenience was foreseeable, and the other person relied on us precisely so that our later moods would not matter. Yet it does not follow that every promise binds in every circumstance. If I promise to meet a friend for lunch and on the way I find a stranger injured on the road, no sensible friend would think I had wronged them by stopping. What releases me is not my changed preference but a change the promise was never meant to cover, one that both of us, had we thought of it, would have agreed to exclude. The force of a promise, then, is real but bounded by what it was understood to be about.",
   "A promise holds against mere inconvenience because others rely on it, but it lapses when circumstances arise that both parties would have agreed it was never meant to cover.",
   [("Because other people plan around what we have promised them, a promise must be kept whatever happens, and only the person to whom it was made can release us from it.",
     "The paragraph says 'it does not follow that every promise binds in every circumstance', and the injured stranger releases the promiser without any release from the friend, so this option contradicts it."),
    ("Stopping to help an injured stranger on the way to a lunch appointment is not a wrong against the friend who is kept waiting at the table.",
     "This is only the illustration; it leaves out the general point that a promise binds against inconvenience yet is bounded by what it was understood to cover."),
    ("A promise binds only for as long as the person who made it still wishes to keep it, since a change of preference is itself a change of circumstances.",
     "The paragraph says the inconvenience 'was foreseeable' and that what releases the promiser is 'not my changed preference', which this option reverses.")],
   "The paragraph makes two linked claims: a promise cannot be cancelled for inconvenience, because the other person relied on it so that 'our later moods would not matter', and yet it is 'bounded by what it was understood to be about', so an unforeseen change both parties would have excluded releases it. Only the correct answer keeps both halves.",
   "Picking the option that keeps only the first half of the argument and turns it into an absolute duty.",
   "V02A-promise-limits", "The two halves of the argument are stated plainly and the distractors are an absolute, a detail and a reversal.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 002
ps("V02-A-002", "Philosophy and ethics", "Effortful virtue is admirable, but effortless virtue is its finest form.", "Medium",
   "Two pictures of moral worth pull in different directions. On one, the person who is honest only by overcoming a strong temptation to lie deserves more credit than the person to whom lying never occurs, since the first has had to do something and the second has merely been lucky in temperament. On the other, the person who must struggle reveals that part of them still wants the wrong thing, while the person for whom honesty is second nature shows what a good character actually looks like. Each picture captures something. The struggler's effort is admirable as an achievement, but we would not wish our friends to remain strugglers for ever; we hope that effort will eventually turn into ease. Effort, on this view, is the honourable path to virtue rather than its finest form.",
   "Overcoming temptation deserves admiration as an achievement, but the ideal is a character for which doing right has become natural, so effort is a route to virtue rather than its peak.",
   [("A person who is honest without any effort deserves little moral credit, since an untroubled temperament is a matter of luck, whereas the struggler has actually had to achieve something difficult.",
     "This is the first of the two pictures the paragraph reports; the conclusion ranks ease above effort, calling effort 'the honourable path to virtue rather than its finest form'."),
    ("Struggling against temptation shows that a person's character is defective, so effortful honesty has no real moral value when it is compared with natural honesty.",
     "The paragraph calls the struggler's effort 'admirable as an achievement' and 'honourable', so denying it any value contradicts the paragraph."),
    ("Since each picture of moral worth captures something true, neither can be preferred to the other, and moral credit must always be judged case by case.",
     "The paragraph does say 'Each picture captures something', but it then ranks them, hoping effort 'will eventually turn into ease', so it does not leave the question open.")],
   "The paragraph sets out two views, grants that each 'captures something', and then reconciles them: effort is admirable but should 'eventually turn into ease', so effort is 'the honourable path to virtue rather than its finest form'. The correct answer captures both the concession and the ranking.",
   "Taking the first reported view, which the paragraph states at length, as the author's conclusion.",
   "V02A-effortless-virtue", "Two reported views precede the author's synthesis; one distractor states a reported view and another stops at the concession.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 003
ps("V02-A-003", "Philosophy and ethics", "Idealised moral thought experiments are weaker evidence against principles than assumed.", "Difficult",
   "Moral philosophers often test principles against imagined cases: a runaway trolley, a surgeon with five dying patients, a drowning child beside an expensive suit. The method is defended on the ground that stripping away messy detail isolates the feature that matters. But the stripping is not neutral. A real surgeon could never be certain that the transplant would succeed, that no one would find out, or that the healthy visitor had no one depending on him; our reluctance to approve the killing may be a sound response to exactly these uncertainties, which the case asks us to set aside but which our intuitions were formed to track. When an intuition about an idealised case conflicts with a principle, it is therefore unclear which has been refuted. This does not make imagined cases useless, since they can still show that a principle has implications its defenders had not noticed; it makes them weaker as evidence against principles than they are usually taken to be.",
   "Intuitions about stripped-down cases may still track real features the case removes, so such cases can expose unnoticed implications of principles but are weaker evidence against them than assumed.",
   [("Because imagined cases strip away the uncertainties that shape real moral judgement, they cannot tell us anything reliable about moral principles and ought to be abandoned by philosophers.",
     "The paragraph says this 'does not make imagined cases useless', since they can reveal implications 'its defenders had not noticed', so abandoning them goes beyond and against it."),
    ("When an intuition about an idealised case clashes with a moral principle, the intuition should be distrusted and the principle retained, because the imagined case is too unrealistic to count.",
     "The paragraph concludes that in such a clash 'it is therefore unclear which has been refuted'; it does not say the principle wins, only that the case is weaker evidence than supposed."),
    ("Our reluctance to approve of a surgeon killing one visitor to save five patients is best explained by our doubts about whether the transplant would really succeed.",
     "This narrows the paragraph to one example and hardens its 'may be a sound response' into a claim about the best explanation, while dropping the general claim about imagined cases as evidence.")],
   "The paragraph argues that idealisation 'is not neutral': intuitions were formed to track real uncertainties the case removes, so a clash between intuition and principle leaves it 'unclear which has been refuted'. It then qualifies: imagined cases are not useless but are 'weaker as evidence against principles than they are usually taken to be'. The correct answer keeps the reason, the concession and the degree.",
   "Choosing the option that says the principle should win, which turns 'unclear which has been refuted' into a verdict.",
   "V02A-thought-experiments", "The tempting distractor draws a one-sided verdict from a symmetric claim of uncertainty; separating them needs the exact force of 'unclear which has been refuted'.",
   exams=DT)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 004
ps("V02-A-004", "Philosophy and ethics", "Enduring institutions can apologise for past wrongs without imposing personal guilt.", "Medium",
   "It is sometimes objected that an institution cannot meaningfully apologise for wrongs committed before any of its present members were born, since none of them did anything wrong. The objection assumes that an apology is mainly a confession of personal guilt. But an apology also does something else: it states publicly that what happened was a wrong, not a misfortune or a regrettable necessity, and it commits the speaker to a relationship in which that wrong is not repeated or excused. A university, a company or a city persists across generations precisely as the bearer of such commitments; it signs contracts that outlive its staff and honours debts its founders incurred. If it can inherit obligations of this kind, it can also inherit the duty to acknowledge what was done in its name. What it cannot do is make its present members feel personal guilt, and an apology that demands such feeling misunderstands its own purpose.",
   "Because an apology also names a wrong publicly and commits to a changed relationship, an enduring institution can apologise for past wrongs, though not impose personal guilt on present members.",
   [("Institutions cannot sincerely apologise for wrongs done before their current members were born, because an apology is above all a confession of personal guilt by the person who committed the wrong.",
     "This is the objection the paragraph reports in order to reject; the paragraph says the objection wrongly 'assumes that an apology is mainly a confession of personal guilt'."),
    ("Universities, companies and cities ought to pay compensation to the descendants of those they wronged, in the same way that they repay the debts their founders incurred.",
     "The paragraph mentions inherited debts only as an analogy for inherited commitments; it says nothing about compensation, so this adds a prescription it does not make."),
    ("An institutional apology succeeds only when it makes the present members of the institution feel personally guilty for what was once done in its name.",
     "The last sentence says an apology 'that demands such feeling misunderstands its own purpose', so this option reverses the paragraph.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the objection by showing that an apology does more than confess guilt: it 'states publicly that what happened was a wrong' and commits to a relationship. Because institutions inherit commitments such as contracts and debts, they can inherit the duty to acknowledge past wrongs, but they cannot make present members 'feel personal guilt'. The correct answer holds the reason, the conclusion and the limit.",
   "Mistaking the reported objection in the first sentence for the paragraph's own position.",
   "V02A-institutional-apology", "The paragraph opens with a view it rejects, and the analogy with debts invites an outside prescription.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 005
ps("V02-A-005", "Psychology", "Ordinary nostalgia is a resource for coping rather than a flight from the present.", "Easy",
   "Nostalgia was once treated as a kind of illness, a homesickness that weakened soldiers and emigrants. A more recent view sees it as doing useful work. People tend to become nostalgic when they feel lonely, uncertain or adrift, and the memories they return to are usually of moments shared with others: a family meal, a school trip, a celebration. Recalling such moments reminds a person that they have been valued and connected, and that sense of continuity can steady them in an unsettled present. Nostalgia is not always benign; it can harden into a refusal to accept change, or into a fantasy of a past that never was. But in its ordinary form it seems less a flight from the present than a way of drawing on the past to cope with it.",
   "Ordinary nostalgia, though it can turn into denial of change or a fantasy past, mostly helps people cope with an unsettled present by recalling times of connection.",
   [("Nostalgia is essentially a retreat from the present, a form of homesickness that leaves people less able to deal with the difficult circumstances they face.",
     "This is the older view the paragraph reports; it concludes that ordinary nostalgia is 'less a flight from the present than a way of drawing on the past to cope with it'."),
    ("People who feel lonely or uncertain are more likely than others to recall the family meals, school trips and celebrations they once shared.",
     "This is a supporting detail; it omits the point that such recollection steadies people and the qualification that nostalgia is 'not always benign'."),
    ("Because it recalls moments of connection, nostalgia is always beneficial, and fears that it encourages resistance to change are without foundation.",
     "The paragraph says nostalgia 'is not always benign' and 'can harden into a refusal to accept change', which this option denies.")],
   "The paragraph contrasts an old view of nostalgia as illness with a view of it as useful, explains how it helps (recalled connection steadies a person), and qualifies the claim: it 'is not always benign'. The correct answer states the main claim with the qualification.",
   "Choosing the 'always beneficial' option and overlooking the qualification in the second-last sentence.",
   "V02A-nostalgia", "The qualification is explicit and each distractor fails on a clearly stated line.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 006
ps("V02-A-006", "Psychology", "Judging decisions by outcomes is sensible under ignorance but persists when it is not needed.", "Medium",
   "When a decision turns out badly, observers tend to judge it as having been a poor decision, even when they are told exactly what the decision-maker knew at the time. A doctor whose reasonable choice of treatment is followed by a rare complication is rated as less competent than one who made the same choice and was lucky. This tendency to grade decisions by their outcomes is often described simply as an error, and in many settings it is one. Yet it is not wholly irrational. In daily life we rarely know what someone knew, and outcomes are often the only evidence we have of their judgement; a rule of thumb that trusts outcomes is a sensible response to that ignorance. The difficulty is that we go on applying the rule even when the facts that would make it unnecessary are placed in front of us.",
   "Grading decisions by results makes sense when we cannot tell what the decision-maker knew, but people keep doing it even once they have been told.",
   [("Judging a decision by its outcome is an irrational error that observers should drop entirely, since outcomes reveal nothing about the quality of a person's choice.",
     "The paragraph says the tendency 'is not wholly irrational' and that outcomes are 'often the only evidence we have', so this contradicts it."),
    ("Doctors whose patients suffer rare complications are unfairly rated as less competent than equally careful colleagues who simply happened to be lucky with their patients.",
     "This is the illustration only; it leaves out why the habit exists and where it goes wrong."),
    ("Because outcomes are usually the best evidence of judgement, people are right to grade decisions by results even when they know what the decision-maker knew.",
     "The last sentence calls exactly this 'The difficulty': applying the rule 'even when the facts that would make it unnecessary are placed in front of us'.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (outcomes colour judgements of decisions), then qualifies the usual verdict that this is simply an error: under ignorance, trusting outcomes is 'a sensible response'. Its point is that the habit misfires when the relevant facts are available. The correct answer holds both the defence and the criticism.",
   "Choosing the option that calls the habit simply irrational, which is the view the paragraph qualifies.",
   "V02A-outcome-bias", "The paragraph both defends and criticises the habit; two distractors each keep only one side.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 007
ps("V02-A-007", "Psychology", "People know their feelings but infer, rather than observe, the causes of their choices.", "Difficult",
   "Ask people why they chose one pair of socks over another, why they liked a job candidate, why they moved house, and they will readily give reasons. It is tempting to treat these reasons as reports, read off from some inner record of what actually moved them. There is good ground for doubt. People's explanations often match what an outsider would guess, given the culture's ordinary theories of why people act, more closely than they match whatever in fact influenced the choice; and when an influence was something they did not notice, such as the position of an item on a shelf, they seldom mention it. It does not follow that people know nothing of their own minds. They are usually right about what they feel and what they are attending to. What is shaky is their access to the causes of their choices, which they seem to infer much as anyone else would, while experiencing the inference as direct knowledge.",
   "People generally know what they feel and attend to, but their accounts of why they chose something seem to be inferences from common theories of behaviour, mistakenly felt as direct insight.",
   [("People have no reliable knowledge of their own minds, since the explanations they offer for their choices merely repeat what any outsider would have guessed about them.",
     "The paragraph says 'It does not follow that people know nothing of their own minds' and that they are 'usually right about what they feel', so this overstates it."),
    ("People knowingly give false reasons for their choices, preferring explanations that fit their culture's theories of action to admitting influences they would rather hide.",
     "The paragraph says people experience their inference 'as direct knowledge' and that the missed influences were ones 'they did not notice', so there is no knowing falsehood or concealment."),
    ("People's explanations of their choices tend to leave out influences they did not notice, such as where an item stood on a shelf, and so are incomplete rather than mistaken.",
     "The paragraph says explanations match common theories 'more closely than they match whatever in fact influenced the choice', so they can misstate causes, not merely omit some; and this option drops the contrast with knowledge of feelings.")],
   "The paragraph doubts that reasons for choices are 'reports' from an inner record, because they track the culture's theories rather than the real influences. It then limits the doubt: people are 'usually right about what they feel'. The shaky part is access to causes, which people 'infer much as anyone else would' while feeling they know directly. The correct answer keeps the limit and the precise claim.",
   "Choosing the 'incomplete rather than mistaken' option, which sounds measured but softens the paragraph's claim into mere omission.",
   "V02A-introspection-limits", "The most tempting distractor is a moderate-sounding distortion; rejecting it needs the contrast between matching outsiders' theories and matching actual causes.",
   exams=DT)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 008
ps("V02-A-008", "Psychology", "Procrastination is mood management rather than poor time management.", "Medium",
   "Procrastination is usually treated as a failure of time management, and the usual remedies follow from that diagnosis: calendars, lists, deadlines broken into smaller pieces. These help some people, but they often fail the chronic procrastinator, who typically knows perfectly well how much time remains and what needs to be done. A better account starts from feeling rather than scheduling. The task that is put off is usually one that provokes some discomfort, whether boredom, self-doubt or fear of being judged, and delay offers immediate relief from that discomfort at the cost of greater trouble later. Seen this way, the procrastinator is not miscalculating time but managing a mood, badly. Planning tools are not useless, but they work best when they also make the task feel less threatening, for instance by turning a vague, daunting assignment into a first step small enough to begin without dread.",
   "Chronic procrastination is less poor time management than an escape from a task's unpleasant feelings, so planning aids help mainly when they also make the task feel less threatening.",
   [("Calendars, lists and deadlines are useless against procrastination, which can be cured only by tackling the fear of being judged that lies behind every task a person keeps delaying.",
     "The paragraph says planning tools 'are not useless', and it names fear of judgement as only one of several discomforts, alongside 'boredom' and 'self-doubt'."),
    ("Procrastinators put off tasks because they underestimate how long the work will take, so breaking deadlines into smaller pieces is the most effective remedy available to them.",
     "This is the time-management diagnosis the paragraph rejects; it says the chronic procrastinator 'knows perfectly well how much time remains'."),
    ("Procrastinators deliberately trade present relief for later trouble after calmly weighing the two, which shows that their habit of delay is a rational choice.",
     "The paragraph says the procrastinator is 'managing a mood, badly', so it does not present delay as a calm and rational weighing of costs.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the time-management diagnosis, offers an account based on feeling (delay relieves the discomfort a task provokes), and concedes that planning tools help when they make the task 'feel less threatening'. The correct answer states the new diagnosis and the qualified role of planning tools.",
   "Choosing the option that dismisses planning tools altogether, missing the concession 'not useless'.",
   "V02A-procrastination-mood", "One distractor states the rejected view and another overstates the new one; the correct answer needs the concession about tools.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 009
ps("V02-A-009", "Society and culture", "Libraries remain valuable as free public space, not only as book lenders.", "Easy",
   "It is often said that public libraries have lost their purpose now that so much information can be found online. The argument mistakes what libraries do. Lending books was always only part of their role; they are also among the few indoor public places where a person may stay for hours without buying anything, where a student without a quiet room at home can study, an elderly reader can find company, and a job seeker can use a computer and ask for help. These functions have not moved online, and for people with little money or space they may matter more than ever. Libraries certainly need to change what they offer, but the case for keeping them does not rest on books alone.",
   "Even with information online, libraries still matter because they also provide free space, room to study, company and practical help that poorer people cannot easily find elsewhere.",
   [("Now that information is freely available on the internet, public libraries have lost most of their purpose and need no longer be maintained by towns and cities.",
     "This is the claim the paragraph opens by rejecting; it says 'The argument mistakes what libraries do'."),
    ("Public libraries should stop lending books altogether and turn themselves entirely into community centres that offer computers, help and social activities.",
     "The paragraph says libraries 'need to change what they offer' and that their case 'does not rest on books alone', not that lending should stop."),
    ("Students who lack a quiet room at home depend on public libraries as a place where they can study undisturbed for long hours.",
     "This is one example from a list; it misses the general point that libraries serve many needs that have not moved online.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the claim that the internet has made libraries pointless, because their role was never only lending: they give free indoor space, study room, company and help, functions that 'have not moved online'. It concedes they must change. The correct answer gives the main point and its reason.",
   "Choosing the option that turns 'need to change' into abandoning books entirely.",
   "V02A-public-libraries", "The paragraph states its point directly after a rejected opening claim.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 010
ps("V02-A-010", "Society and culture", "Humour depends on shared assumptions, so faithful translation may require a new joke.", "Medium",
   "Jokes are notoriously the hardest thing to translate, and the reason is not simply that they rely on puns. Even a joke with no wordplay depends on what its audience already knows and expects: which professions are thought pompous, which towns are thought backward, which subjects are too serious to mention lightly. The joke works by briefly upsetting these expectations, and a listener who does not share them sees nothing upset. A translator can explain the background, but an explained joke has lost the speed on which its effect depends. The more faithful strategy is often to replace the joke with a different one that does similar work for the new audience, accepting that fidelity to the effect may require infidelity to the words.",
   "Humour resists translation because it plays on an audience's shared expectations, which explanation cannot supply without killing the joke, so translators may need to substitute new jokes.",
   [("Jokes are hard to translate mainly because puns depend on the sounds and double meanings of words in one language, which rarely survive the move into another.",
     "The paragraph says the reason is 'not simply that they rely on puns'; even jokes 'with no wordplay' depend on shared expectations."),
    ("A good translator of humour should add brief notes explaining the cultural background of each joke, since readers from another culture cannot otherwise see why it is funny.",
     "The paragraph says 'an explained joke has lost the speed on which its effect depends', so it recommends replacement rather than explanation."),
    ("No text of any kind can be translated faithfully, because every piece of writing depends on assumptions that are shared only by its original audience.",
     "The paragraph is about jokes, and it allows a kind of fidelity ('fidelity to the effect'), so this is both too broad and too absolute.")],
   "The paragraph explains why humour is hard to translate: jokes upset expectations that only the original audience holds, and explaining them destroys their speed. It concludes that substituting a joke that 'does similar work' is often more faithful. The correct answer holds the cause and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the puns option, which repeats the explanation the paragraph says is incomplete.",
   "V02A-translating-humour", "One distractor gives the explanation the paragraph sets aside and another recommends the strategy it rejects.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 011
ps("V02-A-011", "Society and culture", "Conservation usually beats restoration, but some marked reconstruction keeps the past legible.", "Medium",
   "Two attitudes have long divided those who care for old buildings. One holds that a ruined church or fort should be restored to the state its builders intended, replacing lost towers and repainting faded walls, so that visitors see the work as it was meant to be seen. The other holds that such restoration is a kind of forgery: the building's age, its damage and its later alterations are part of its history, and the honest course is to stop further decay while leaving the marks of time visible. The second attitude has largely prevailed among conservation specialists, and with reason, since reconstruction often rests on guesswork about what the original looked like. Yet it is not costless. A building conserved as a fragment may be honest but unintelligible to visitors who cannot picture the whole, and some careful, clearly marked reconstruction may be the price of making the past legible.",
   "Conserving old buildings as found is usually more honest than restoring them, though some limited and clearly marked reconstruction may be needed so that visitors can understand the whole.",
   [("Restoring old buildings to their original appearance is a form of forgery and should never be attempted, since any reconstruction of a lost part rests on guesswork.",
     "The paragraph ends by allowing 'some careful, clearly marked reconstruction', so a ban on all reconstruction contradicts its qualification."),
    ("Old buildings should be restored to the state their builders intended, because visitors cannot appreciate a structure that has been left standing as a ruined fragment.",
     "The paragraph says the conservation attitude has prevailed 'and with reason'; it accepts only limited reconstruction, not full restoration as the rule."),
    ("Specialists disagree about whether to restore or to conserve old buildings, and since each approach has real costs as well as benefits, neither can be preferred as a general rule.",
     "The paragraph does not leave the matter open: it says conservation has prevailed 'with reason' and treats reconstruction as an exception justified by legibility.")],
   "The paragraph compares restoration and conservation, sides with conservation 'with reason' (reconstruction is often guesswork), and then concedes a cost: fragments can be 'unintelligible', so some clearly marked reconstruction may be justified. The correct answer gives the preference and the concession.",
   "Choosing the 'never be attempted' option and ignoring the concession in the last sentence.",
   "V02A-building-restoration", "The author's preference and concession must both be kept, and the 'neither preferred' option is a tempting false balance.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 012
ps("V02-A-012", "Society and culture", "Printing folk songs preserved them but froze a fluid tradition into a false standard.", "Difficult",
   "Collectors who travelled villages writing down folk songs are usually praised for saving a heritage that would otherwise have vanished. The praise is deserved, but it hides a paradox. A song in oral circulation has no single correct version: each singer alters the words, the tune and the length to suit the occasion, and this variation is not corruption but the way the tradition lives. Once a collector prints one version, often the one sung by the most cooperative informant on a particular afternoon, that version tends to become the standard, taught in schools and performed at festivals, while the others come to seem like mistakes. The act of preservation thus fixes what was fluid. None of this means collectors should have stayed at home; without them most of these songs would be lost entirely. It means that a printed collection should be read as a snapshot of a moving practice, not as the authentic original from which variants departed.",
   "Collecting folk songs saved them, but making one often arbitrary version the standard hid the variation that was the tradition itself, so printed texts should be read as snapshots, not originals.",
   [("Folk song collectors did more harm than good to the traditions they studied, since printing a single version of each song destroyed the variation that kept them alive.",
     "The paragraph says the praise 'is deserved' and that without collectors 'most of these songs would be lost entirely', so it does not judge them to have done more harm than good."),
    ("Collectors of folk songs ought to have printed every variant they heard in a village, because a single version cannot fairly represent a tradition that changes with each singer.",
     "The paragraph's remedy concerns how printed collections 'should be read', not how collectors should have worked; recording every variant is a prescription it never makes."),
    ("Variation among singers gradually corrupted folk songs, so collectors were right to fix the most authentic version of each song in print before it was lost.",
     "The paragraph says variation 'is not corruption but the way the tradition lives' and that printed versions should not be read as 'the authentic original', so this reverses it.")],
   "The paragraph praises collectors, then identifies a paradox: printing one version, often chosen by chance, made it the standard and made other versions 'seem like mistakes', fixing 'what was fluid'. It qualifies the criticism (collectors were still needed) and draws a conclusion about reading: a collection is 'a snapshot of a moving practice'. The correct answer keeps the praise, the paradox and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the option about printing every variant, which sounds like a natural remedy but is not the one the paragraph draws.",
   "V02A-folk-song-collecting", "The strongest distractor offers a plausible remedy of its own; it must be rejected because the paragraph's conclusion is about reading, not collecting.",
   exams=DT)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 013
ps("V02-A-013", "History and politics", "Memoirs are revealing but must be checked against contemporary records because of hindsight.", "Easy",
   "Historians value memoirs for their vividness but treat them with caution, and the reasons are not mainly about honesty. A memoir is usually written long after the events, when the author knows how things turned out, and memory tends to reshape the past to fit that knowledge: doubts are forgotten, lucky guesses become foresight, and decisions made in confusion acquire a clear purpose. Memoirists also write for an audience and often to defend a reputation. None of this makes memoirs worthless. They record impressions, motives and atmosphere that official papers omit, and read alongside letters and documents from the time, they can be revealing. The caution is about using them alone as a record of what happened and why.",
   "Because memoirs are shaped by hindsight and the wish to persuade, historians value their vivid detail but test them against records from the time instead of relying on them alone.",
   [("Historians distrust memoirs chiefly because their authors usually lie on purpose, inventing events in order to protect their own reputations from the criticism of later writers.",
     "The paragraph says the reasons for caution 'are not mainly about honesty'; the main problem is memory reshaped by hindsight."),
    ("Since memory reshapes the past to fit what came later, memoirs are worthless as historical evidence and should be ignored in favour of official papers written at the time.",
     "The paragraph says 'None of this makes memoirs worthless' and that they record what 'official papers omit'."),
    ("Memoirs written long after the events they describe tend to present lucky guesses as foresight and confused decisions as having had a clear purpose from the start.",
     "This is one detail of how hindsight works; it leaves out the paragraph's balance between the value of memoirs and the caution in using them alone.")],
   "The paragraph explains why historians are cautious with memoirs (hindsight reshapes memory; authors write to persuade), then says memoirs are still valuable when 'read alongside letters and documents from the time'. The caution is 'about using them alone'. The correct answer captures both sides.",
   "Choosing the option about deliberate lying, which the first sentence rules out as the main reason.",
   "V02A-memoir-evidence", "Each distractor contradicts or narrows a clearly stated sentence.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 014
ps("V02-A-014", "History and politics", "Counterfactual reasoning is built into causal history; only long speculative chains are idle.", "Medium",
   "Counterfactual history, the study of what might have happened had some event gone differently, is often dismissed as a parlour game for historians with too much time. The dismissal overlooks that every causal claim in history is already a counterfactual. To say that a drought caused a rebellion is to say that without the drought the rebellion would not have occurred, or would have come later or smaller; a historian who refuses to think about alternatives cannot weigh causes at all. The real objection is to counterfactuals that stray far from the moment in question, imagining chains of consequences decades long, where every step multiplies uncertainty. Asking what was genuinely possible for the people involved, given what they knew and could do, is not fantasy but part of explaining why events took the course they did.",
   "Thinking about alternatives is unavoidable in assigning historical causes and is legitimate when kept to options open at the time; only long chains of imagined consequences deserve dismissal.",
   [("Counterfactual history is an idle pastime, because what did not happen cannot be studied and so tells us nothing about the real causes of events that did happen.",
     "This is the dismissal the paragraph opens by rejecting; it says the dismissal 'overlooks that every causal claim in history is already a counterfactual'."),
    ("Since every causal claim involves a counterfactual, historians should freely imagine how the world would have developed over many decades had key events gone otherwise.",
     "The paragraph says the 'real objection' is to counterfactuals 'imagining chains of consequences decades long', so this recommends exactly what it criticises."),
    ("A historian who claims that a drought caused a rebellion is implicitly committed to the view that without the drought the rebellion would not have happened as it did.",
     "This restates the paragraph's illustration only; it omits both the general claim and the limit placed on legitimate counterfactuals.")],
   "The paragraph defends counterfactual history against dismissal by showing that causal claims already imply alternatives. It then locates the legitimate objection: long chains where 'every step multiplies uncertainty'. Asking what was 'genuinely possible for the people involved' is part of explanation. The correct answer gives the defence and its limit.",
   "Choosing the 'freely imagine over decades' option, which takes the defence without its limit.",
   "V02A-counterfactual-history", "The defence and its limit must be held together; one distractor extends the defence past the limit.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 015
ps("V02-A-015", "History and politics", "For boundaries and jurisdiction, maps often created what they seemed to record.", "Medium",
   "We tend to think of a map as a picture of what is already there, accurate or inaccurate according to how faithfully it copies the land. Many maps in history did more than copy. When a state surveyed a frontier region and drew a line across it, the line often did not record a boundary that people on the ground recognised; it created one, which taxes, passports and armies would later enforce. Rivers that had joined communities became borders that separated them. To say this is not to deny that maps can be more or less accurate about coastlines and mountains. It is to recognise that for things like boundaries, ownership and jurisdiction, the map was frequently an instrument for making the world rather than a record of it.",
   "Maps may copy physical features well or badly, but for boundaries and jurisdiction they often created the divisions they seemed only to record.",
   [("Maps are never faithful copies of the land, since every map, including those of coastlines and mountains, reflects the political aims of the state that commissioned it.",
     "The paragraph says 'this is not to deny that maps can be more or less accurate about coastlines and mountains', so this overstates and contradicts it."),
    ("When states surveyed and mapped frontier regions, rivers that had once connected neighbouring communities were often turned into borders that divided them, later enforced with taxes and passports.",
     "This is one illustration; it misses the general claim that maps of boundaries were 'an instrument for making the world rather than a record of it'."),
    ("Historical maps of frontier regions were frequently inaccurate, because the surveyors sent by states misrecorded the boundaries that local people already recognised on the ground.",
     "The paragraph's point is that the lines often 'did not record a boundary that people on the ground recognised' at all; the issue is creation, not inaccurate recording.")],
   "The paragraph challenges the idea that a map is only a copy: for boundaries, ownership and jurisdiction, the line 'created' the division that taxes and armies then enforced. It concedes that maps of physical features can be judged by accuracy. The correct answer keeps the concession and the main claim.",
   "Choosing the 'frequently inaccurate' option, which keeps the copy model the paragraph rejects.",
   "V02A-maps-make-borders", "One distractor stays inside the copy model while sounding critical of maps; the reader must see that the paragraph rejects that model for boundaries.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 016
ps("V02-A-016", "History and politics", "Archives are selective but, read against their grain, reveal more of ordinary life than intended.", "Difficult",
   "Historians have learned to read archives not only for what they say but for what they leave out. An archive is the residue of an administration's interests: it keeps tax rolls, court cases and correspondence among officials, and so it records people mainly when they paid, offended or petitioned. Whole areas of life, such as household work, friendship and private belief, appear only when they collided with authority. It has become common to conclude that the archive simply silences the powerless. That conclusion goes too far. The same court records that reduce a person to a defendant often preserve, in the words of witnesses, details of daily life recorded nowhere else, and a petition written to flatter an official can still reveal what its writer wanted. The archive is biased in what it selects, but read against its grain it yields more about ordinary lives than its makers ever intended.",
   "Archives record ordinary people mainly where they met authority, yet saying they simply silence the powerless goes too far, since records read against their purpose reveal much about daily life.",
   [("Because archives keep only what served an administration's interests, they silence the powerless, and their records cannot tell historians much about ordinary people's lives.",
     "This is the 'common' conclusion the paragraph reports and then rejects with 'That conclusion goes too far'."),
    ("Archives are far less biased than historians have come to assume, since court records and petitions represent ordinary people as fully as they represent officials.",
     "The paragraph says plainly that 'The archive is biased in what it selects'; its claim is that biased records still yield more than intended, not that they are unbiased."),
    ("Court records are more useful than petitions to historians of daily life, because witnesses in court speak more freely than people who are writing in order to flatter an official.",
     "The paragraph uses both kinds of record as examples of the same point and never ranks one above the other, so this comparison is outside its scope.")],
   "The paragraph accepts that archives are 'the residue of an administration's interests' and so biased, then rejects the stronger conclusion that 'the archive simply silences the powerless': read 'against its grain', the archive yields details its makers never meant to keep. The correct answer holds the bias, the rejected overstatement and the reason.",
   "Choosing the 'far less biased' option, which overcorrects the rejected conclusion into a denial of bias.",
   "V02A-archive-silences", "The paragraph rejects an overstatement without denying the underlying bias; two distractors err in opposite directions.",
   exams=DT)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 017
ps("V02-A-017", "Economics and business", "Loyalty programmes chiefly raise the cost of switching and gather data for sellers.", "Easy",
   "Loyalty programmes look like a gift: buy nine coffees and the tenth is free. For the seller, the point is less the discount than the switching cost it creates. A customer who is halfway to a reward has a reason to return to the same shop even when another is closer or cheaper, because going elsewhere means abandoning the progress already made. The programme also gives the seller information about what customers buy and how often, which can be used to target offers. Customers are not necessarily fooled; many value the rewards and would shop in the same place anyway. But the programme is designed mainly to make leaving a little more expensive than staying.",
   "Loyalty schemes chiefly serve sellers by making it costlier for customers to switch, while also gathering purchase data, even if many customers benefit from them too.",
   [("Loyalty programmes are mainly a way for shops to reward their regular customers with discounts, out of gratitude for their continued custom over time.",
     "The paragraph says that for the seller 'the point is less the discount than the switching cost it creates', so gratitude is not the main purpose."),
    ("Loyalty programmes trick customers into paying more than they otherwise would, so shoppers who join them always end up worse off than those who do not.",
     "The paragraph says customers 'are not necessarily fooled' and that many 'value the rewards', so this is too extreme."),
    ("Shops use the information collected through loyalty cards to find out what their customers buy and how often, and then send them targeted offers.",
     "This is a secondary point; the paragraph's main claim, stated in its last sentence, is about making 'leaving a little more expensive than staying'.")],
   "The paragraph explains what loyalty programmes do for sellers: they create a switching cost and collect purchase data. It qualifies the point (customers 'are not necessarily fooled') and ends by restating the main purpose. The correct answer keeps the purpose, the secondary benefit and the qualification.",
   "Choosing the data option, which is true but secondary to the switching cost.",
   "V02A-loyalty-programmes", "The main purpose is stated twice and the distractors fail on explicit lines.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 018
ps("V02-A-018", "Economics and business", "Zero prices disable caution, letting services charge in diffuse costs such as attention and data.", "Medium",
   "A price of zero is not just a very low price. People respond to free offers far more strongly than a small drop in price would predict: a product reduced from a small sum to nothing attracts many more takers than one reduced by the same amount to a slightly smaller sum. One reason is that a free choice seems to carry no possible loss, so the usual weighing of costs and benefits is skipped. This is why many online services charge nothing and earn their revenue from advertising or data instead. The user does pay, in attention and in information about themselves, but these costs are spread out and hard to count, and so they do not trigger the caution that a visible price, however small, would.",
   "Free offers draw people disproportionately because zero seems to carry no risk of loss, letting services charge in attention and data, real costs too diffuse to prompt caution.",
   [("Online services that charge nothing are genuinely free to their users, since what users hand over in exchange, such as their attention, has no real value to them.",
     "The paragraph says 'The user does pay, in attention and in information about themselves', so it denies that such services are genuinely free."),
    ("A product whose price is cut from a small sum to nothing gains many more buyers than one whose price is cut by the same amount but stays above zero.",
     "This is the opening observation only; it leaves out the explanation and the point about online services charging in hidden costs."),
    ("Governments ought to require online services to charge a small but visible price, so that users would weigh the hidden costs of their attention and data more carefully.",
     "The paragraph explains a phenomenon and makes no recommendation about regulation, so this adds a prescription it does not contain.")],
   "The paragraph explains why a zero price has a special pull (a free choice 'seems to carry no possible loss', so weighing is skipped) and applies this to online services, whose real costs in attention and data are 'spread out and hard to count'. The correct answer holds the mechanism and its application.",
   "Choosing the opening observation, which is true but omits the explanation and the paragraph's point about hidden costs.",
   "V02A-zero-price", "The correct answer must join a mechanism to its application; one distractor gives the observation alone and another adds a policy.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 019
ps("V02-A-019", "Economics and business", "Physical closeness yields interaction only where work demands it and people feel unobserved.", "Medium",
   "Open-plan offices were promoted on the promise that removing walls would bring colleagues together and multiply the chance conversations from which ideas emerge. When companies have tracked what actually happens after such a move, the picture has often been the reverse: people conscious of being overheard send more messages and speak less face to face, wearing headphones to create the privacy that walls once gave. It would be wrong, however, to conclude that open plans always reduce collaboration. Teams whose work requires constant quick coordination, such as a newsroom or a trading floor, may genuinely benefit. The lesson is that physical closeness does not by itself produce interaction; whether it does depends on what the work requires and on whether people feel free to talk without being observed.",
   "Open plans often cut face-to-face talk, though not for every team, since closeness yields interaction only where work demands it and people feel unobserved.",
   [("Open-plan offices always reduce collaboration among colleagues, because employees who fear being overheard retreat behind headphones and communicate only through written messages.",
     "The paragraph says 'It would be wrong, however, to conclude that open plans always reduce collaboration', and it names teams that 'may genuinely benefit'."),
    ("Bringing colleagues physically closer together reliably multiplies the chance conversations from which ideas emerge, which is why open plans suit most companies better than walled offices.",
     "This is the original promise, which the paragraph says has often turned out 'the reverse'."),
    ("Newsrooms and trading floors gain from open-plan layouts because their teams need to coordinate constantly and quickly, unlike most office workers, who would prefer walls.",
     "This is the exception the paragraph mentions, not its point, and the claim about what most workers prefer is not in the paragraph.")],
   "The paragraph reports that open plans have often reduced face-to-face talk, qualifies this (some teams 'may genuinely benefit'), and draws the lesson that closeness produces interaction only depending on the work and on whether people feel 'free to talk without being observed'. The correct answer keeps the finding, the qualification and the lesson.",
   "Choosing the 'always reduce' option, which keeps the finding but drops the qualification.",
   "V02A-open-plan-offices", "The paragraph reports a finding, qualifies it and generalises; the distractors each keep one stage.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 020
ps("V02-A-020", "Economics and business", "Reputation enforces informal credit cheaply but only in dense communities, excluding newcomers.", "Easy",
   "In many markets, small traders lend goods to one another on credit without any written contract, and the arrangement works far better than an outsider might expect. The reason is that these traders deal with each other repeatedly and talk constantly. A trader who fails to repay does not merely lose one partner; word spreads, and soon no one in the market will extend credit to them. The threat of losing that reputation enforces the debt more cheaply than a court could. The system has limits. It works only within a community dense enough for news to travel, and it tends to exclude newcomers, who have no reputation to lose and therefore little to offer as security.",
   "Informal credit among traders works because repeated dealing and gossip make a lost reputation a strong penalty, but it holds only in close-knit groups and tends to shut out newcomers.",
   [("Informal credit based on reputation is cheaper and more reliable than legal contracts, and so it should replace the courts as the main way of enforcing debts everywhere.",
     "The paragraph says the system 'works only within a community dense enough for news to travel', so recommending it everywhere ignores its stated limits."),
    ("A trader who fails to repay a debt in a close-knit market soon finds that no other trader there is willing to sell goods to them on credit.",
     "This is one step in the explanation; it leaves out why the system works overall and the limits the paragraph sets on it."),
    ("Because they have no bad record behind them, newcomers to a market find it easier than established traders to obtain goods on credit without a contract.",
     "The paragraph says the system 'tends to exclude newcomers, who have no reputation to lose', which is the reverse of this option.")],
   "The paragraph explains why credit without contracts works among traders (repeated dealing and talk make reputation an enforcement device) and then states its limits: it needs a dense community and excludes newcomers. The correct answer gives the mechanism and the limits.",
   "Choosing the option about a defaulting trader, which is a step in the argument rather than its point.",
   "V02A-reputation-credit", "The mechanism and the limits are both stated in plain sentences.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 021
ps("V02-A-021", "Economics and business", "Omitting unpaid household work from national income is a flaw, but pricing it brings its own distortion.", "Difficult",
   "National income accounts leave out the cooking, cleaning and care done unpaid within households, and the omission is often called a simple bias that a better statistic would correct. The criticism is right that something valuable goes unmeasured, and that this can distort judgements: when a family starts paying for childcare it once provided itself, measured output rises though little more care may be given. But the remedy is less simple than it sounds. To add household work to the accounts one must price it, and every method of pricing an hour of unpaid care, whether at what a hired carer would earn or at what the carer could have earned elsewhere, imposes a market valuation on activity whose meaning to those who do it is often precisely that it is not done for money. The omission is a real flaw, but the obvious fix may trade one distortion for another.",
   "Leaving unpaid household work out of national income is a genuine and misleading flaw, yet including it would require market prices that misrepresent such work, so the fix may bring a distortion of its own.",
   [("Unpaid household work cannot be valued in money at all, so national income accounts are right to leave it out, and the critics of the omission are simply mistaken.",
     "The paragraph says 'The criticism is right that something valuable goes unmeasured' and calls the omission 'a real flaw', so it does not defend leaving the work out."),
    ("Leaving out unpaid household work is a simple bias in national income figures, which statisticians could correct by pricing each hour at what a hired carer would earn.",
     "This is the view the paragraph reports and then qualifies with 'the remedy is less simple than it sounds'; it treats the hired-carer rate as one of the methods that impose a market valuation."),
    ("When families begin to pay for childcare they previously provided themselves, measured national output rises even though little additional care may actually be given to the children.",
     "This is the paragraph's illustration of the distortion; it omits the main point that the obvious remedy has a problem of its own.")],
   "The paragraph agrees with critics that omitting household work is a flaw that 'can distort judgements', then argues that the remedy is not simple: any price for unpaid care 'imposes a market valuation' on work valued because 'it is not done for money'. It concludes that the fix 'may trade one distortion for another'. The correct answer states both the flaw and the problem with the fix.",
   "Choosing the option that treats the omission as a simple bias with an easy fix, which is the view the paragraph qualifies.",
   "V02A-unpaid-work-accounts", "The author agrees with a criticism but rejects its remedy; the distractors take either side too far or keep only the example.",
   exams=DT)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 022
ps("V02-A-022", "Science and environment", "Introduced species should be judged by their effects, not their origin.", "Easy",
   "Any species that arrives in a new region with human help is now often labelled invasive and treated as a threat to be removed. Some newcomers deserve that reputation: a predator introduced to an island can wipe out birds that evolved without defences against it. But many introduced plants and animals settle in without causing measurable harm, and some come to fill roles left empty by the loss of native species, providing food for pollinators or shelter for birds. Judging a species by its origin rather than its effects can waste money on removing harmless arrivals while drawing attention from the damaging ones. The useful question is not where a species came from but what it actually does.",
   "Introduced species should be judged by their effects rather than their origin, since while some are destructive, many are harmless or even useful, and blanket removal wastes effort.",
   [("Introduced species are generally good for the regions they reach, so efforts to remove them do more harm than good and should be given up altogether.",
     "The paragraph says 'Some newcomers deserve that reputation', such as island predators, so it does not oppose all removal."),
    ("Predators that people introduce to remote islands can wipe out native birds that evolved without any means of defending themselves against such attackers.",
     "This is the paragraph's example of a harmful newcomer; it leaves out the main point about judging species by their effects."),
    ("Because they arrive with human help rather than naturally, introduced species should be removed as soon as they are found in a new region.",
     "This is the attitude the paragraph criticises; it says 'The useful question is not where a species came from but what it actually does'.")],
   "The paragraph criticises treating every introduced species as a threat. It concedes that some are harmful, notes that many are harmless or useful, and concludes that species should be judged by 'what it actually does'. The correct answer captures the conclusion and its basis.",
   "Choosing the option that all introduced species are good, which ignores the concession about harmful predators.",
   "V02A-introduced-species", "The point is stated in the last sentence and the distractors fail on explicit lines.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 023
ps("V02-A-023", "Science and environment", "Filtering results by excitement distorts the scientific record without any individual dishonesty.", "Easy",
   "Journals are more likely to publish studies that find an effect than studies that find none, and researchers, knowing this, are less likely to write up their null results at all. Each choice seems reasonable, since a finding that nothing happened looks less interesting than a discovery. Together, however, they distort the record. If twenty teams test a useless remedy and one obtains a positive result by chance, that one study may be the only one anyone reads, and the remedy will appear to work. The problem lies not in any single dishonest scientist but in a system that filters results by whether they are exciting rather than by whether they are sound.",
   "Because journals and researchers both favour positive findings over null ones, the published record can make useless treatments look effective, a fault of the system rather than of dishonest individuals.",
   [("Scientists frequently falsify their data in order to obtain positive results, since journals refuse to publish any study that fails to find an effect for its treatment.",
     "The paragraph says the problem lies 'not in any single dishonest scientist', and it says journals are 'more likely' to publish positive results, not that they refuse all others."),
    ("When many research teams test a useless remedy, one of them is likely to obtain a positive result purely by chance, and that study may be the one that is widely read.",
     "This is the illustration; it omits the cause the paragraph identifies, the shared preference for positive findings."),
    ("Journals should be required by law to publish every study submitted to them, whatever its results, so that null findings are no longer lost to other researchers.",
     "The paragraph diagnoses a problem and makes no proposal, least of all a legal one, so this adds a prescription.")],
   "The paragraph explains how two individually reasonable choices, journals preferring positive results and researchers not writing up null ones, together 'distort the record', and it places the fault in 'a system that filters results by whether they are exciting'. The correct answer gives the cause, the effect and the point about the system.",
   "Choosing the falsification option, which the last sentence explicitly rules out.",
   "V02A-publication-bias", "The point is stated in the last sentence; distractors are a distortion, the example and a prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 024
ps("V02-A-024", "Science and environment", "Seed banks should back up, not replace, conservation of wild plant populations.", "Medium",
   "Two strategies compete for the limited money available to protect plant diversity. Seed banks store frozen samples of thousands of species in secure vaults, offering insurance against extinction at modest cost. Conservation in the wild protects plants where they grow, along with the insects, fungi and soils they depend on. The seed bank's great advantage is security, but its samples are frozen in time: a population kept in a vault stops adapting to changing climate and pests, and some species have seeds that cannot survive drying and freezing at all. Wild populations go on evolving, but they are exposed to every threat the vault avoids. Neither strategy can substitute for the other. The vault is best understood as a backup for living populations, not a replacement for them.",
   "Seed banks are secure but halt adaptation and miss some species, while wild populations evolve but stay exposed, so vaults should back up, not replace, wild conservation.",
   [("Since seed banks protect their samples from every threat at modest cost, they are a more efficient way to safeguard plant diversity than conservation in the wild.",
     "The paragraph says samples in vaults stop adapting and that some seeds cannot be stored, and it concludes that 'Neither strategy can substitute for the other'."),
    ("Because frozen seeds stop adapting and some cannot be stored at all, money spent on seed banks would be better spent entirely on protecting wild habitats instead.",
     "The paragraph calls the vault 'a backup for living populations' and notes that wild populations are 'exposed to every threat the vault avoids', so it does not favour dropping seed banks."),
    ("Some plant species produce seeds that cannot survive being dried and frozen, so these species cannot be kept in seed banks and must be conserved in the wild.",
     "This is one detail among the weaknesses of seed banks; it misses the comparison and the conclusion that the two strategies complement each other.")],
   "The paragraph compares two strategies, giving each an advantage and a weakness: vaults are secure but static and incomplete, wild populations evolve but are exposed. It concludes that the vault is 'a backup for living populations, not a replacement for them'. The correct answer holds the comparison and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing one of the one-sided options because the paragraph's criticism of seed banks seems to favour the wild, or its praise of security seems to favour the vault.",
   "V02A-seed-banks", "The paragraph balances two strategies; two distractors each take one side, and one is a detail.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 025
ps("V02-A-025", "Science and environment", "Many disputes over species boundaries are about definitions, though classification is not arbitrary.", "Medium",
   "Naturalists have long divided into those who tend to lump similar organisms into one broad species and those who split them into many narrow ones. The dispute is often presented as a quarrel of temperaments, as if one side were simply careless and the other fussy. In fact it reflects something real about living things. Populations diverge gradually, and at any moment some pairs are clearly distinct, some clearly the same, and many lie in between, interbreeding a little or differing in a few traits. For these intermediate cases no fact of nature settles whether there are one species or two; the answer depends on which criterion one adopts. This does not make classification arbitrary, since the clear cases are clear. But it means that some disputes about species are disagreements about definitions rather than about the organisms.",
   "Because populations diverge gradually, many borderline species cases depend on the criterion chosen, so some lumper and splitter disputes concern definitions, though clear cases prevent arbitrariness.",
   [("Since populations diverge gradually, the division of living things into species is entirely arbitrary and reflects only the preferences of the naturalists who classify them.",
     "The paragraph says 'This does not make classification arbitrary, since the clear cases are clear', which this option contradicts."),
    ("Naturalists who lump organisms into broad species tend to be careless, while those who split them into many narrow species are excessively fussy about small differences.",
     "This is the 'quarrel of temperaments' picture that the paragraph reports and then corrects with 'In fact it reflects something real about living things'."),
    ("Disagreements between lumpers and splitters could be settled if naturalists studied the disputed organisms more closely and gathered more evidence about how often and how freely they interbreed.",
     "The paragraph says that for intermediate cases 'no fact of nature settles' the question, so more evidence about the organisms would not resolve a dispute about definitions.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the view that the lumper and splitter dispute is about temperament: gradual divergence leaves many intermediate cases where 'the answer depends on which criterion one adopts'. It limits the claim (clear cases keep classification from being arbitrary) and concludes that some disputes are 'about definitions rather than about the organisms'. The correct answer keeps the reason, the conclusion and the limit.",
   "Choosing the 'more evidence' option, which assumes the dispute is factual when the paragraph says it is partly definitional.",
   "V02A-lumpers-splitters", "One distractor treats a definitional dispute as factual, which requires grasping the paragraph's central distinction.")
